  "phase3_gemini_self_healing": {
    "forensics": "The losing cluster is primarily driven by two factors: tight stop-loss whipsaws (0.45% min_sl_pct) during mid-morning consolidation (10:15\u201311:05 IST) and severe fee drag on premature breakeven stops and late-afternoon entries (14:25\u201314:30 IST). Specifically, trades like AXISBANK (#8) and COALINDIA (#9) achieved positive gross P&L but flipped to net losses due to transaction costs, while TATASTEEL (#2, #3) suffered immediate stop-outs from noise within normal intraday volatility bands.",
    "learning": "In Indian NSE cash equities on a micro-capital base, a 0.45% stop-loss sits well inside the standard 5-minute bid-ask and retail noise envelope; widening the stop floor while demanding higher volume confirmation and extending the breakeven trigger is essential to survive mid-morning liquidity sweeps and overcome statutory fee drag.",
